  "heat": 3,
  "text": "Jael ended a war with a tent peg and a bottle of milk. Judges chapter four. Sisera is the commander of nine hundred iron chariots, and he has just watched his entire army fall to Barak. So he runs. On foot. And he runs to the tent of Jael, wife of Heber the Kenite, because there was peace between his king and that household. He thinks he has found a neutral corner. Jael goes out to meet him and says, turn in, my lord, turn in to me, fear not. He comes in. She covers him with a mantle. He asks for a little water, and she opens a bottle of milk and gives him a drink, and covers him again. Then he tells her to stand in the door, and if anyone asks whether a man is there, say no. So he sleeps. The text says he was fast asleep and weary. And Jael takes a nail of the tent and a hammer, and goes softly, and it is finished. Then she walks out and shows Barak the body. Deborah's song in the next chapter calls her blessed above women in the tent. Scripture does not soften this one, and neither will I.",
